const title = 'How to File for Divorce in Abu Dhabi: Expat and Muslim Routes';
const description =
  'How expats file for divorce in Abu Dhabi: the Civil Family Court route for non-Muslims, the ADJD Personal Status Court for Muslims, documents, fees, appeals.';

const hreflang = {
  en: 'https://divorceu.ae/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-abu-dhabi/',
};

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: 'https://divorceu.ae/en/' },
  { name: 'How to File for Divorce in Abu Dhabi', url: 'https://divorceu.ae/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-abu-dhabi/' },
];

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: title,
  description,
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Aleksi Suon', url: 'https://divorceu.ae/en/author/aleksi-suon/' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Divorce UAE' },
  datePublished: '2026-07-04',
  dateModified: '2026-10-12',
  inLanguage: 'en-AE',
  mainEntityOfPage: 'https://divorceu.ae/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-abu-dhabi/',
};

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: 'How do I file for divorce in Abu Dhabi?',
    answer:
      'It depends on which route applies to you. Non-Muslim expats normally file at the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court: the no-fault divorce application is submitted at a typing centre, the fee is AED 5,000, there is no Family Guidance stage and the divorce is granted at the first hearing. Muslim couples, including Muslim expats, file at the ADJD Personal Status Court under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, where the judge may refer them to Family Guidance first (it was mandatory under the old 2005 law).',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court?',
    answer:
      'It is a dedicated court for non-Muslims in Abu Dhabi, citizens and foreigners, set up under Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021 with the 2022 Regulation (ADJD Chairman’s Decision No. 8 of 2022). It applies civil, no-fault rules rather than Sharia, uses bilingual Arabic and English forms, can run the whole case in English, and handles civil marriage, divorce, joint custody, post-divorce financial claims, wills and inheritance. It is separate from the ADJD Personal Status Court that hears Muslim cases.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How long does a divorce take in Abu Dhabi?',
    answer:
      'At the Civil Family Court the ADJD says it aims to finalise the case within one month of registration, and the 2022 Regulation sets the divorce hearing at least 30 days after the application is approved (Article 14), with the divorce granted at that first hearing. Financial claims after the divorce take longer if an accounting expert is appointed. At the Personal Status Court there is no fixed timeline; law-firm guides indicate roughly three to six months for an uncontested case and a year or more for a contested one.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Do I need to be a UAE resident to file for divorce in Abu Dhabi?',
    answer:
      'For the Civil Family Court you need a connection to Abu Dhabi rather than a set period of residence. The court hears civil family cases where the parties have a domicile, residence, or current or former place of work in the emirate, and a civil divorce where Abu Dhabi is the residence, workplace or domicile of either spouse (Article 4 of the 2022 Regulation). The ADJD’s page summarises this as Abu Dhabi residents or anyone who married at the Civil Family Court. The Regulation sets no minimum residence period. Muslim cases follow Article 3 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, which looks at where the parties live or work and where the marital home is.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What documents do I need to file for divorce in Abu Dhabi?',
    answer:
      'For a civil no-fault divorce the ADJD lists a copy of the marriage certificate in English and Arabic and copies of both passports and Emirates IDs. English documents do not need an Arabic translation at the Civil Family Court (Article 44 of the 2022 Regulation). At the Personal Status Court foreign documents need a legal Arabic translation and, if issued abroad, attestation. For custody, maintenance or asset claims you will also need children’s birth certificates, salary certificates, bank statements and any settlement agreement, plus a power of attorney if a lawyer acts for you.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How much does a divorce cost in Abu Dhabi?',
    answer:
      'The civil no-fault divorce application at the Civil Family Court costs AED 5,000 (Article 13 of the 2022 Regulation). At the Personal Status Court the ADJD shows the fee before you register, and its FAQ lists alimony cases and family guidance cases among those exempt from court fees. Lawyer fees are not regulated; as an indicative range from law-firm cost guides, an uncontested case runs roughly AED 8,000 to 25,000, and contested cases start near AED 40,000.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can a Muslim expat divorce under their home country’s law in Abu Dhabi?',
    answer:
      'Possibly. Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 applies to non-UAE citizens unless one spouse insists on applying his or her own law, or another law they agree on that is permitted (Article 1(3)). The case is still heard by the ADJD Personal Status Court. Take advice first, because the choice can change rights on khula, custody and money.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I appeal a Civil Family Court judgment?',
    answer:
      'Only in limited cases for money claims. Under Article 49 of the 2022 Regulation, a judgment on alimony or post-divorce financial rights is final and enforceable as soon as it is issued if the amount is under AED 500,000, and such a final judgment can be challenged only for breach of the rules of jurisdiction or because the judgment or the procedure was void (Article 50). Personal Status Court judgments can be appealed within 30 days (Article 9 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024).',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I get a divorce in ADGM or DIFC in Abu Dhabi?',
    answer:
      'No. ADGM is a common-law financial free zone whose courts hear civil and commercial cases; its family-related role is limited to non-Muslim wills and succession, not granting divorces. The DIFC Courts are in Dubai and have no divorce, custody or maintenance jurisdiction either. For a non-Muslim divorce in Abu Dhabi, the correct venue is the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court, not ADGM or DIFC.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court faster than the Personal Status Court?',
    answer:
      'For non-Muslims, generally yes. The Civil Family Court grants a no-fault divorce at the first hearing with no Family Guidance stage, and the ADJD says it aims to close cases within about a month. At the Sharia-track Personal Status Court the judge may first refer the case to Family Guidance, and in contested harm cases arbitrators can take up to 60 days from their appointment (Article 72 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024).',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  lang="en"
  dir="ltr"
  hreflang={hreflang}
  schema={schema}
  lastmod="2026-10-12"
>
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="container hero__inner">
      <div class="hero__content">
        <Breadcrumb items={breadcrumbs} lang="en" />
        <h1 class="hero__h1">How to File for Divorce in Abu Dhabi: Expat and Muslim Routes</h1>
        <p class="byline">By Aleksi Suon, UAE Expat &amp; Legal Consultant · Updated October 2026</p>
        <p class="hero__sub">
          Expats in Abu Dhabi divorce through one of two courts: non-Muslims normally use the Civil
          Family Court, where the divorce is granted at the first hearing for an AED 5,000 application
          fee and the case can run in English, while Muslims, expats included, use the ADJD Personal
          Status Court under the federal 2024 law. This guide shows which route applies to you, then
          walks through both step by step: documents, fees, timelines and appeals. For the statutes
          behind it, see our <a href="/en/uae-divorce-law/">UAE divorce law guide</a>.
        </p>
        <ul class="hero__trust">
          <li>✓ Expat and Muslim routes covered</li>
          <li>✓ ADJD &amp; Civil Family Court</li>
          <li>✓ Documents, fees &amp; appeals</li>
          <li>✓ Free legal consultation</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="hero__form">
        <LeadForm lang="en" variant="hero" heading="Free Divorce Consultation" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="container main-content">
    <figure style="margin:0 0 8px;">
      <img src="/images/divorce-abu-dhabi-court.webp" alt="Filing for divorce in Abu Dhabi: a modern Abu Dhabi courthouse building" width="1200" height="675" loading="eager" decoding="async" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px;" />
    </figure>

    <!-- Overview -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="overview-heading">
      <h2 id="overview-heading">Divorce in Abu Dhabi: Two Routes, One Judicial Department</h2>
      <p>
        Everything family-related in the emirate runs through the <strong>Abu Dhabi Judicial
        Department (ADJD)</strong>, but which court you file in depends on your religion and the law
        you fall under. Muslim couples use the Abu Dhabi Personal Status Court under
        <strong>Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024</strong> (in force 15 April 2025, which fully
        replaced the old Law No. 28 of 2005). Non-Muslim foreigners have a second option that no
        other emirate pioneered in the same way: the <strong>Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court</strong>,
        created under <strong>Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021</strong> with executive Regulation No. 8
        of 2022.
      </p>
      <p>
        The two routes are genuinely different in speed and procedure, so picking the right one
        matters. The figures below are indicative and drawn from the current law and law-firm cost
        guides, not an official fee schedule.
      </p>
      <div class="stats-grid">
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">2</span>
          <span class="stat-label">separate divorce routes in Abu Dhabi</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">~1</span>
          <span class="stat-label">month target at the Civil Family Court</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">1st</span>
          <span class="stat-label">session in which a civil divorce can be granted</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">EN + AR</span>
          <span class="stat-label">Civil Family Court works in both</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="info-box info-box--blue" style="margin-top:24px;">
        <h3>The differentiator in one line</h3>
        <p>
          For non-Muslims, the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court grants a no-fault divorce at the first
          hearing (Article 12 of the 2022 Regulation), with no Family Guidance stage (Article 39),
          no iddah rules in the civil law, and proceedings that can run entirely in English (Article
          44). Compare the two emirates in our
          <a href="/en/dubai-vs-abu-dhabi-divorce/">Dubai vs Abu Dhabi divorce</a> guide, and for how
          the court itself works, see our <a href="/en/family-court-abu-dhabi/">Abu Dhabi family court
          guide</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Which route -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="route-heading">
      <h2 id="route-heading">Expats in Abu Dhabi: Which Route Applies to You</h2>
      <p>
        Religion and nationality decide your route, not your visa. Non-Muslim expats normally divorce
        at the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court; Muslim expats divorce at the ADJD Personal Status Court.
        A wrong assumption about which court hears your case is the most common reason expats lose
        weeks in Abu Dhabi, so start here.
      </p>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="info-box info-box--gold">
          <h3>Non-Muslim expats: Civil Family Court</h3>
          <p>
            Governed by Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021 and its 2022 Regulation (and, at federal level,
            <a href="/en/non-muslim-divorce-uae/">Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022</a>). Either
            spouse can file without giving a reason, there is no Family Guidance stage, the divorce is
            granted at the first hearing, the application fee is AED 5,000, the case can run in
            English, and joint custody lasts until the child is 16 (Article 25 of the 2022 Regulation).
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-box info-box--blue">
          <h3>Muslim expats: Personal Status Court</h3>
          <p>
            Governed by Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, which applies to non-UAE citizens unless one
            spouse insists on applying his or her own law (Article 1(3)). Forms include talaq, khula,
            judicial divorce on grounds and annulment. The judge may refer the case to Family Guidance
            first (Article 8). Proceedings are in Arabic, and custody runs to 18 (Article 123).
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <h3>Who the civil court covers</h3>
      <p>
        Article 5 of the 2022 Regulation lists who the civil rules apply to: non-Muslim UAE citizens;
        foreigners who hold the nationality of a country that does not mainly apply Islamic law in
        family matters, according to a list in the ADJD's guidance manual (for dual nationals, the
        nationality used for UAE residence counts); couples who married in such a country; and couples
        who married under the civil marriage rules. The ADJD's Civil Family Court page adds that
        citizens of Arab League member states may be asked for an official document proving religion.
        If you married at the court itself, see our <a href="/en/civil-marriage-abu-dhabi/">civil
        marriage in Abu Dhabi guide</a>.
      </p>
      <h3>Residence and jurisdiction</h3>
      <p>
        There is no minimum residence period in the 2022 Regulation. What matters is a link to the
        emirate: the court hears civil family cases where the parties have a domicile, residence, or
        current or former place of work in Abu Dhabi (Article 4). Even if the respondent lives abroad,
        it keeps jurisdiction over a civil divorce where Abu Dhabi is the residence, workplace or
        domicile of either spouse, over civil marriages concluded in the emirate, and where the wife
        is the claimant and lives or works there. The ADJD's own page summarises eligibility for a
        no-fault divorce as Abu Dhabi residents or anyone who married at the Civil Family Court.
      </p>
      <h3>Mixed or unclear cases</h3>
      <p>
        If one spouse is Muslim, or you hold the nationality of, or married in, a country that applies
        Islamic family law, the civil route may not be open to you and the Personal Status Court may be
        the correct venue. A foreign couple can ask that their home-country law be applied where one of
        them insists on it (Article 1(3) of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024). Take advice before you file: the civil court must decline a case on its own
        initiative if it lacks jurisdiction (Article 4 of the 2022 Regulation). Our guide to
        <a href="/en/divorce-for-expats-in-uae/">divorce for expats in the UAE</a> covers the wider
        choices, including divorcing in your home country.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Route A: Civil Family Court -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="civil-heading">
      <h2 id="civil-heading">Route A: Filing at the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court (Non-Muslims)</h2>
      <p>
        This is the standout Abu Dhabi option for non-Muslim expats. Under Law No. 14 of 2021, the
        Civil Family Court grants divorce on the request of either spouse without needing to prove
        fault, harm or grounds (Article 6), at the first hearing after the case is registered, with no
        referral to Family Guidance (Articles 7 and 15). The ADJD says the court aims to finalise the
        case within one month of registration. All forms and procedures are bilingual in Arabic and
        English (Article 17), and the court may run the whole case in English if the parties do not
        speak Arabic (Article 44 of the 2022 Regulation).
      </p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Confirm eligibility and gather documents</h3>
          <p>
            Check you qualify for the civil route (see the section above) and are connected to Abu
            Dhabi by residence, domicile, work or a civil marriage concluded there. The ADJD lists the
            core documents as a copy of the marriage certificate in English and Arabic, and copies of
            both parties' passports and Emirates IDs. See the full
            <a href="/en/documents-for-divorce-uae/">documents for divorce checklist</a>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Submit the application at a typing centre</h3>
          <p>
            The ADJD's Civil Family Court page says the no-fault divorce application is submitted at
            one of the typing centres in Abu Dhabi, on the bilingual application form. It must be
            submitted by you or by a special representative (Article 13 of the 2022 Regulation), and
            the prescribed fee is AED 5,000. A lawyer can lodge it for you under a power of attorney if
            you cannot attend. For video hearings, electronic service and tracking a case from abroad,
            see <a href="/en/online-divorce-uae/">online divorce in the UAE</a>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Approval, notice and the defence form</h3>
          <p>
            The court checks the application, especially its jurisdiction. Once it is approved, the
            divorce hearing is set at least 30 days after approval and both spouses are notified
            (Article 14). Urgent requests in the file go to the judge the day after registration. The
            other spouse may file a defence form, mainly on jurisdiction, no later than 10 working days
            before the hearing.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>First hearing and divorce judgment</h3>
          <p>
            At the hearing the judge issues the divorce after confirming that the applicant attended
            and still wants the divorce (Article 14(6)). No evidence of why the marriage broke down is
            needed (Article 12). Joint custody of any children is set automatically in the divorce
            ruling (Article 26).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Deal with custody, alimony and assets</h3>
          <p>
            Joint custody applies until the child is 16 unless a parent waives it or asks the court to
            remove the other (Articles 25, 29 and 32); our guide to
            <a href="/en/child-custody-abu-dhabi/">child custody in Abu Dhabi</a> covers schedules,
            travel and relocation. If you cannot settle finances, the wife can apply for alimony after
            the divorce judgment (Article 8 of Law No. 14 of 2021), and either spouse can be ordered to
            pay the other a lump sum (Article 16 of the 2022 Regulation). While that is decided, a
            divorced wife can ask for temporary alimony on six months of bank statements (Article 21);
            see <a href="/en/temporary-alimony-uae/">temporary alimony in the UAE</a>. When estimating
            the financial compensation due after divorce, Article 18 lets the court and its accounting
            expert be guided by a cash figure of no less than 25% of the husband's last monthly income
            multiplied by the years of marriage, alongside his property and any joint account. It is a
            guide the court can raise or lower, not a cap, and it is specific to Abu Dhabi. See
            <a href="/en/alimony-abu-dhabi/">spousal maintenance in Abu Dhabi</a> for how awards are
            worked out.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Appeal limits on money judgments</h3>
          <p>
            A judgment on alimony or post-divorce financial rights for less than AED 500,000 is final
            and enforceable as soon as it is issued (Article 49), and can be challenged only for a
            breach of the rules of jurisdiction or because the judgment or the procedure was void
            (Article 50). Prepare your financial evidence for the first round, because there may not be
            a second.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Collect and, if needed, attest the decree</h3>
          <p>
            The divorce is recorded at the point of judgment. To use the decree abroad, it usually
            needs attestation by the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs and legalisation for the
            destination country. Our
            <a href="/en/divorce-in-uae/">divorce in UAE overview</a> covers what happens next with
            visa status and finances.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="cta-inline">
        <p>Not sure the civil route fits your case?</p>
        <a href="#contact" class="btn btn--primary">Get a Free Assessment</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Route B: Personal Status Court -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="sharia-heading">
      <h2 id="sharia-heading">Route B: Filing at the Abu Dhabi Personal Status Court (Muslims)</h2>
      <p>
        Muslim divorces in Abu Dhabi, for Emiratis and Muslim expats alike, follow the Sharia track
        under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024. Compared with the civil route, the judge may put a
        Family Guidance stage at the front, proceedings are in Arabic, and the process is closer to
        the procedure used across the other emirates.
      </p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Get legal advice and confirm your grounds</h3>
          <p>
            Confirm whether your case is uncontested or contested and what outcomes are realistic on
            custody, maintenance and assets. Under the 2024 law, recognised forms include talaq,
            khula (Article 65), judicial divorce on grounds such as harm (Article 71), failure to
            maintain (Article 77) or a spouse's addiction (Article 80), and annulment. Muslim expats
            should also decide early whether either spouse will insist on their home-country law
            (Article 1(3)).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Register the case; Family Guidance if the judge refers it</h3>
          <p>
            You register the case through a typing centre or ADJD Smart Services and bring your
            marriage certificate, passports, Emirates IDs and proof of residence. Under Article 8 of
            the 2024 law the supervising judge may refer the spouses to Family Guidance if a settlement
            looks feasible (under the 2005 law referral was mandatory). Urgent and temporary orders on
            alimony and custody, and cases to prove a divorce, are not referred, and the law sets no
            fixed period for the stage.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Settlement or return to court</h3>
          <p>
            A counsellor meets both spouses. A settlement is recorded in a report signed by both
            spouses and the counsellor, approved by the supervising judge and enforceable like a
            court order (Article 8(2)). If there is no settlement, the case goes on to the court. In
            contested divorces for harm or discord the court can appoint two arbitrators, whose work
            the 2024 law caps at 60 days from their appointment (Article 72; it was 90). None of this
            applies to the civil route.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Register for hearings and file your claims</h3>
          <p>
            With the referral letter, the case is registered for court hearings. This is where you
            lodge claims for custody, child support, spousal maintenance and, where relevant,
            division of assets and debts.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Court hearings</h3>
          <p>
            Uncontested cases often need a single substantive hearing. Contested cases run through
            several hearings over months, sometimes with a court-appointed accountant assessing
            finances or an expert on custody.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Divorce decree and registration</h3>
          <p>
            The court issues the judgment. If the husband pronounced talaq outside court, he must
            document it before the court within 15 days (Article 58); if he does not, without an
            excuse the court accepts, the wife is entitled to compensation equal to her alimony from
            the date of the divorce to the date it is documented. The duty does not make an
            undocumented talaq void, and the wife can file her own case to prove the divorce. Appeals
            in personal status matters must be filed within 30 days (Article 9).
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Custody under the 2024 law now continues to age 18 for both boys and girls (Article 123), and a
        child aged 15 or older may choose which parent to live with unless the child's interest
        requires otherwise (Article 122).
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Side by side -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="compare-heading">
      <h2 id="compare-heading">Civil Family Court vs Personal Status Court: Side by Side</h2>
      <p>
        The practical differences are what make Abu Dhabi distinctive. Here is how the two routes
        stack up.
      </p>
      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Civil Family Court (non-Muslim)</th><th>Personal Status Court (Muslim)</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Governing law</td><td>Abu Dhabi Law 14/2021 + Reg 8/2022</td><td>Federal Decree-Law 41/2024</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Who uses it</td><td>Non-Muslims, citizens and expats (Reg. Art. 5)</td><td>Muslims, citizens and expats (Art. 1)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Basis</td><td>Civil, no-fault</td><td>Islamic Sharia</td></tr>
            <tr><td>How to apply</td><td>Form at a typing centre; AED 5,000 fee (Reg. Art. 13)</td><td>Typing centre or ADJD Smart Services; fee shown at registration</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Family Guidance / reconciliation</td><td>No (Law Art. 15, Reg. Art. 39)</td><td>Only if the judge refers the case (Art. 8)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Language of proceedings</td><td>Bilingual; can run fully in English (Reg. Art. 44)</td><td>Arabic</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Iddah waiting period</td><td>No iddah rules in the civil law</td><td>Applies (Arts. 81 to 85)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Timeline (uncontested)</td><td>Hearing at least 30 days after approval; ADJD aims for about 1 month</td><td>About 3 to 6 months (indicative)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Custody default</td><td>Joint custody to 16 (Reg. Arts. 25, 26)</td><td>Custody to 18, choice at 15 (Arts. 122, 123)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Appeal</td><td>Money judgments under AED 500,000 final on issue; limited grounds (Reg. Arts. 49, 50)</td><td>30 days (Art. 9)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">
          The Personal Status Court timeline is indicative, from law-firm guides; real timelines depend
          on the court's diary and whether the case is contested. See our full
          <a href="/en/divorce-timeline-uae/">UAE divorce timeline guide</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Documents -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="docs-heading">
      <h2 id="docs-heading">Documents You Will Need</h2>
      <p>
        Missing or incorrectly attested paperwork is a common cause of delay in Abu Dhabi. Gather
        these before you file. At the Civil Family Court, expats do not have to translate English
        documents into Arabic (Article 44 of the 2022 Regulation). At the Personal Status Court,
        foreign documents need a legal Arabic translation and, if issued abroad, attestation.
      </p>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="info-box info-box--blue">
          <h3>Core documents</h3>
          <p>
            Marriage certificate (for the civil court, a copy in English and Arabic; for the Personal
            Status Court, if issued abroad, attested and translated); both spouses' passports and visa
            pages; both Emirates IDs; and proof of Abu Dhabi residence or connection to the emirate.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-box info-box--gold">
          <h3>Case-specific documents</h3>
          <p>
            Children's birth certificates and school records for custody; salary certificates, bank
            statements and title deeds for maintenance and asset claims; any prenuptial or settlement
            agreement; and a notarised power of attorney if a lawyer will act for you.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The exact list varies by court, route and whether your case is contested. Our
        <a href="/en/documents-for-divorce-uae/">documents for divorce in the UAE</a> guide breaks
        down attestation and translation requirements in detail.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Costs -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="costs-heading">
      <h2 id="costs-heading">Cost of Divorce in Abu Dhabi (Indicative)</h2>
      <p>
        Two official figures are fixed. The civil no-fault divorce application at the Civil Family
        Court costs AED 5,000 (Article 13 of the 2022 Regulation). At the Personal Status Court the
        ADJD tells you the fee before you complete registration, and its FAQ lists alimony cases and
        family guidance cases among those exempt from court fees; an interim order in the Personal
        Status Division costs AED 100, with alimony requests exempt. Lawyer fees are not regulated:
        the ranges below are indicative figures from law-firm cost guides and vary widely by case and
        firm.
      </p>
      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Divorce type</th><th>Lawyer fees (indicative)</th><th>Typical timeline</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Civil no-fault (uncontested)</td><td>AED 8,000 to 25,000</td><td>About 1 month (ADJD aim)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Uncontested / mutual (Sharia route)</td><td>AED 8,000 to 25,000</td><td>3 to 6 months</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Contested (custody or assets)</td><td>AED 40,000 to 80,000</td><td>1 to 3 years</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Complex (business valuation, international custody)</td><td>AED 150,000+</td><td>1 to 3 years or more</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">
          Indicative only, from law-firm cost guides. Hourly rates run roughly AED 750 to 1,000
          (junior), AED 1,200 to 1,800 (mid-level) and AED 2,000 to 2,500 (senior partner). Many
          lawyers offer a free initial consultation. See our
          <a href="/en/divorce-cost-uae/">full cost of divorce guide</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="cta-inline">
        <p>Worried about costs?</p>
        <a href="#contact" class="btn btn--primary">Get a Free Quote</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Filing from abroad -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="poa-heading">
      <h2 id="poa-heading">Filing From Abroad: Power of Attorney</h2>
      <p>
        You do not always have to be physically in Abu Dhabi to divorce here. If you have left the
        country or cannot attend in person, you can appoint a licensed UAE lawyer through a power of
        attorney to prepare and lodge the application and represent you at hearings.
      </p>
      <p>
        The power of attorney generally needs to be notarised where you sign it, authenticated in
        that country (in apostille countries this first step is often an apostille), then legalised
        by the UAE embassy or consulate there and attested by the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs,
        and translated into Arabic for the Personal Status Court (the Civil Family Court accepts
        English). An apostille on its own is not enough, because the UAE is not a party to the Hague
        Apostille Convention. Once that is
        in place, a <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-abu-dhabi/">divorce lawyer in Abu Dhabi</a> can
        handle most steps, though some stages may still require your appearance or that of your
        representative.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ADGM / DIFC note -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="adgm-heading">
      <h2 id="adgm-heading">A Note on ADGM and DIFC</h2>
      <p>
        Abu Dhabi hosts the ADGM free zone, and some marketing sites frame it as a family-court
        venue. It is not. ADGM is a common-law financial free zone whose courts hear civil and
        commercial cases; its family-related role is limited to non-Muslim wills and succession, not
        granting divorces. The DIFC Courts are in Dubai and have no divorce, custody or maintenance
        jurisdiction either. For a non-Muslim divorce in Abu Dhabi, the correct venue is the Abu Dhabi
        Civil Family Court under Law No. 14 of 2021.
      </p>
      <div class="info-box info-box--gold" style="margin-top:8px;">
        <h3>Where non-Muslims actually divorce in Abu Dhabi</h3>
        <p>
          The Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court, not ADGM. If you have seen a reference to an
          <a href="/en/adgm-divorce-abu-dhabi/">ADGM divorce</a>, read that guide for what ADGM does
          and does not do before you rely on it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

